Pike 9.1: Changes since Pike 9.0
----------------------------------------------------------------------

New language features
---------------------

o Syntax for default values in multi-assignment.

  It is now possible to specify default values in multi-
  assignment.

    [int a, int b = a + 4] = ({ 1 });

  or

    [int a, int b = a + 4] = ({ 1, UNDEFINED });

  Both of the above statements will assign the value 1 to
  the variable a, and the value 5 to the variable b.

  *CAVEAT* Multi-assign in function calls is broken in combination with
  default values (Issue #10169).


Bug fixes
---------

o Compiler

  - Inheritance can now delay a derived module's final compilation until an
    inherited program's compilation unit has finished. This avoids some
    loading-order-dependent failures when modules refer to each other's
    types. The base must already have completed its first pass; unresolved
    placeholders and cyclic final-pass dependencies are still rejected.
    Pike 9.0 and earlier compatibility modes retain the old restrictions.

  - Keep mutable container construction visible to the normal optimizer.
    This restores array append optimizations and avoids replacing simple
    array allocation with expensive recursive copies of constants.

o Runtime

o Operator functions


